>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/training/">Workouts & Training</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Training</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Are Treadmills Bad for Your Knees?</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        For most healthy people, no — and running in general is not the knee-wrecker its reputation suggests. Where treadmills do cause knee trouble, it is almost always how they are used rather than the machine itself.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 6, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#running-and-knees" class="btn-ghost">What the Research Says</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Treadmills are not inherently bad for your knees. For healthy people, the research on
          recreational running and knee arthritis is broadly reassuring, and a treadmill deck is,
          if anything, slightly more forgiving than pavement. What does cause knee problems — on
          a treadmill or anywhere else — is doing too much too soon, repeating an identical
          stride for hours with no variation, poor shoes, steep decline settings and leaning on
          the rails. Those are all things you control.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What this page is, and is not.</strong> We review
          treadmills; this site does not give medical advice, and nothing here is a diagnosis or
          a treatment plan. If you have knee pain, a diagnosed joint condition, a recent injury
          or surgery, or symptoms such as swelling, locking or the knee giving way, please see a
          doctor or physical therapist before using a treadmill — they can assess your knee,
          which a review site cannot.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#running-and-knees">What the Research Says About Running and Knees</a></li>
            <li><a href="#treadmill-vs-outdoors">Treadmill vs Running Outside: Is One Kinder to Knees?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-causes-problems">What Actually Causes Knee Problems on a Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-decline">Incline, Decline and Your Knees</a></li>
            <li><a href="#shoes">Shoes Matter More Than the Deck</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reducing-risk">How to Use a Treadmill Kindly on Your Knees</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-to-see-clinician">When to See a Doctor or Physical Therapist</a></li>
            <li><a href="#other-options">Who Might Prefer Walking, or a Different Machine</a></li>
            <li><a href="#buying">If You Are Buying a Treadmill With Your Knees in Mind</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Bottom Line</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Are treadmills bad for your knees?</strong> For most people with healthy
            knees, no. Walking and running on a treadmill load the knee in essentially the same
            way as walking and running outside, and the evidence does not suggest that
            recreational running wears knees out. A treadmill deck typically has a little more
            give than concrete or asphalt, which some people find more comfortable.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Is running on a treadmill bad for you?</strong> Also no, for most people. It
            is a convenient, controllable way to get the cardiovascular benefits of walking or
            running. The things that make it go wrong — increasing mileage too quickly, never
            varying speed or incline, running in <a href="/how-often-to-replace-running-shoes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>worn-out shoes</a>, holding the handrails — are
            training habits rather than properties of the machine. The wider question is answered in <a href="/is-running-on-a-treadmill-bad-for-you/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is running on a treadmill bad for you</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where it gets more nuanced</strong> is for people who already have knee pain,
            arthritis or a past injury. For them, the treadmill can be a genuinely useful tool,
            because it allows precise control of speed and gradient — but the right starting
            point should come from a clinician, not from a general article. The sections below
            explain the evidence, the specific habits worth avoiding, and the signs that mean you
            should get a knee looked at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-and-knees" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What the Research Says About Running and Knees</h2>
<p>
            The idea that running inevitably wears out knees is widespread and, for recreational
            runners, not well supported. Cartilage is living tissue that responds to load, and a
            body of research suggests that moderate, regular loading is compatible with — and may
            even support — healthy joints.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most frequently cited piece of evidence is a 2017 systematic review and
            meta-analysis that pooled studies of runners and non-runners. It found that hip and
            knee osteoarthritis was less common among recreational runners than among
            sedentary people — roughly 3.5% compared with about 10% in the pooled data — while
            elite and competitive runners with very high training volumes had a higher rate.
            Studies of this kind show associations rather than proving cause, and the people who
            keep running for decades may differ in other ways from those who do not. But they are
            hard to square with the idea that running itself is the problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two caveats matter. First, this research is about people without existing joint
            disease. If you already have osteoarthritis, a meniscus tear or a ligament injury,
            the question is different and belongs with your doctor or physical therapist.
            Second, running does carry a real risk of overuse injuries — knee pain is among the
            most common complaints in runners — but those are mostly about training load, not
            about the knee wearing out.
          </p>
          <p>
            The short version: for healthy adults, the evidence points toward running in
            sensible amounts being fine for the knees. The machine underneath you is a smaller
            factor than how much, how fast and how suddenly you increase what you do on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="treadmill-vs-outdoors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill vs Running Outside: Is One Kinder to Knees?</h2>
<p>
            Biomechanics research comparing treadmill and overground running generally finds the
            two very similar, with small differences in things like stride length and how the
            foot lands that vary from person to person. There is no strong evidence that either
            surface is clearly better or worse for knees in healthy runners.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where the treadmill has an edge.</strong> Most home treadmills have some form
            of deck suspension — elastomer cushions or similar — so the surface gives slightly
            underfoot compared with concrete. The surface is also perfectly even: no kerbs,
            potholes, cambered roads or tree roots to turn an ankle or twist a knee on. And you
            can control speed and incline precisely, which makes gradual progression easy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where it has a drawback.</strong> That same evenness means every stride is
            essentially identical. Outdoors, small variations in surface, gradient and pace
            spread the load across tissues slightly differently. On a belt at a fixed speed, you
            repeat the same movement thousands of times in a session, which may concentrate
            stress on the same structures.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is worth adding that softer is not automatically better. A very soft or bouncy
            deck can make the stride feel less stable and does not necessarily reduce the load on
            the knee in a meaningful way — your body adjusts its stiffness to the surface. Our
            explainer on <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a> covers what deck suspension does and does not do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-causes-problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Actually Causes Knee Problems on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            When people develop knee pain from treadmill use, it is usually one of a small number
            of causes, and almost all of them are habits that can be changed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Too much, too soon.</strong> The single most common culprit. A new treadmill
            and a burst of motivation often produce a jump from not much to daily runs in a
            couple of weeks. Muscles adapt to new training quickly; tendons, cartilage and bone
            adapt more slowly. Increasing distance, duration or intensity gradually — and
            changing one variable at a time — gives them time to catch up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Monotony.</strong> Every session at the same speed, the same gradient and the
            same duration means the same repetitive gait, day after day. Varying speed and
            incline across the week, mixing walking with running, and including some
            cross-training spreads the load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Overstriding.</strong> Reaching forward with a long step and landing on a
            straight leg well in front of the body tends to increase braking forces through the
            knee. Slightly shorter, quicker steps with the foot landing nearer to under the hips
            is a common recommendation for runners with knee complaints, though any change in
            form should be gradual. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a> covers <a href="/treadmill-running-form/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>running form</a> on a belt in more detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Holding the handrails.</strong> Gripping the rails, particularly on a steep
            incline, changes posture and stride in ways that are not natural, and can twist the
            body or tip the pelvis. If you need the rails to keep up, the setting is too high.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Worn or unsuitable shoes.</strong> Covered in its own section below, because
            it is one of the easiest things to fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Incline, Decline and Your Knees</h2>
<p>
            Incline changes how the knee is loaded, and it is worth understanding in both
            directions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking uphill</strong> at a moderate gradient is often a lower-impact
            alternative to running, because it raises effort without raising speed or impact.
            Many people with sore knees find incline walking more comfortable than running at a
            similar effort. However, steep inclines also increase how much the knee bends and how
            hard the thigh muscles work, and some people — particularly those with pain at the
            front of the knee, around the kneecap — find steep gradients aggravating. If
            incline walking hurts, a lower gradient is usually the answer rather than pushing
            through.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking or running downhill</strong>, which some treadmills offer as a decline
            setting, is harder on the knees than it looks. Going downhill requires the thigh
            muscles to work as brakes while lengthening, which loads the front of the knee more
            and is a well-known cause of muscle soreness. <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Decline training</a> has its uses for
            runners preparing for hilly races, but it is not a gentle option. If you are new to
            training or have any knee discomfort, it is reasonable to leave decline alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever the gradient, change it in small steps rather than big jumps, and lower the
            speed as the incline rises. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline percent vs degrees</a> explains what the numbers on the console mean in practice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shoes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Shoes Matter More Than the Deck</h2>
<p>
            The shoe is the part of the system between your foot and the belt, and it is one of
            the variables you can change most easily. A few practical points.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use walking or running shoes</strong>, not cross-trainers, court shoes or
            casual trainers, and never socks or bare feet on a motorised belt. Choose a pair that
            feels comfortable from the first step; comfort is a reasonable guide, and there is
            no single shoe type that suits everyone's knees.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Replace worn shoes.</strong> Midsole foam loses its properties over time.
            A commonly quoted range is somewhere around 300 to 500 miles of running, though it
            varies with the shoe and the runner. If your shoes are visibly compressed or creased
            in the midsole, or the tread is worn unevenly, they are probably due for retirement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Change shoes gradually.</strong> Switching abruptly to a very different
            shoe — much lower, much more minimal, or with a very different drop — changes how
            load is distributed through the lower leg and knee. If you change style, ease into
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you have persistent knee or foot problems, a physical therapist or podiatrist
            can assess whether a particular type of shoe or insert is appropriate for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reducing-risk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Use a Treadmill Kindly on Your Knees</h2>
<p>
            None of this requires special equipment. It is mostly about how you plan your week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Build up slowly.</strong> Increase total weekly time or distance gradually,
            and change one thing at a time — duration, frequency, speed or incline, not several
            at once. A structured plan such as the <a href="/couch-to-5k-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">couch to 5k treadmill programme</a> does this for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vary your sessions.</strong> Mix easy walks, brisk walks, incline walks and,
            if you run, easy runs at different speeds. The <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts guide</a> lays out how to combine session types across a week.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep most sessions easy.</strong> A large share of overuse problems come from
            doing every session hard. Easy sessions at a conversational effort build fitness with
            far less strain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warm up and cool down.</strong> A few minutes of easy walking before and after
            costs nothing and is built into the <a href="/treadmill-workout-plan/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill routine</a> anyway.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Strengthen the muscles around the knee.</strong> Strength work for the thighs
            and hips is commonly recommended for runners and walkers, and is a standard part of
            how physical therapists manage many kinds of knee pain. A physical therapist can
            suggest exercises that suit you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Let go of the rails.</strong> If you need them, turn down the speed or incline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-see-clinician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to See a Doctor or Physical Therapist</h2>
<p>
            Some aches after starting a new exercise routine are normal and settle within a day
            or two. Other symptoms deserve a professional opinion rather than more time on the
            belt. Stop treadmill sessions and get your knee assessed if you notice:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Swelling in or around the knee</li>
            <li>The knee locking, catching or giving way</li>
            <li>Pain that gets worse as you exercise, rather than easing after the warm-up</li>
            <li>Pain that persists for more than a few days, or that wakes you at night</li>
            <li>Pain following a fall, twist or other specific incident</li>
            <li>Being unable to put full weight on the leg</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Also check with a clinician before starting if you have diagnosed arthritis, a
            previous knee injury or surgery, or a condition that affects your joints. Many people
            with these conditions can and do use treadmills — walking is commonly recommended for
            people with knee osteoarthritis — but the appropriate speeds, gradients and volumes
            are individual, and a physical therapist can set them.
          </p>
          <p>
            This page cannot tell you what is causing your pain. A clinician who can examine your
            knee can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Might Prefer Walking, or a Different Machine</h2>
<p>
            For some people, running is simply not the right tool, at least for now. That might
            include people with existing knee arthritis, those returning from injury or surgery,
            people with significant pain at the front of the knee, and those who are carrying a
            lot of extra weight and are new to exercise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking</strong> on a treadmill — including incline walking at a moderate
            gradient — delivers much of the cardiovascular benefit with considerably less impact
            than running. For many people, a brisk incline walk is the most knee-friendly hard
            session available. Our guide to <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmills</a> covers machines suited to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>An elliptical</strong> removes the impact phase entirely, because your feet
            never leave the pedals. It is a common alternative for people whose knees object to
            running, though some find the fixed movement path uncomfortable in its own way. Our
            comparison of <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a> looks at the trade-offs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cycling and swimming</strong> are also very low impact, if you have access to
            them. None of these options is better than the others for everyone; the best one is
            the one your knees tolerate and that you will keep doing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>If You Are Buying a Treadmill With Your Knees in Mind</h2>
<p>
            We have a separate buying guide for this, so we will not repeat it here. In brief:
            the features that matter for sensitive knees are less about maximum softness and more
            about a stable, consistently supported deck, a good incline range, fine speed control
            at low speeds, and a belt long enough that you are not shortening your stride to stay
            on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for bad knees</a> covers specific machines and explains why we favour firmer, well-supported decks over
            the softest available. If you are a beginner with no particular knee issues, the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for beginners</a> is the more relevant starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Treadmills are not bad for healthy knees.</strong> Research on recreational
            runners is broadly reassuring about knee arthritis, treadmill and outdoor running are
            biomechanically similar, and a treadmill deck is a little more forgiving than
            pavement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How you use it matters more than the machine.</strong> Increase training
            gradually, vary your sessions, avoid overstriding, go easy on decline, let go of the
            rails and replace worn shoes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already have knee pain or a joint condition</strong>, get advice from a
            doctor or physical therapist first. Walking, incline walking or an elliptical may be a
            better place to start, and the right amount is individual.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes That Make Knees Hurt on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p>None of these is about the machine. They are about how it gets used, and each one is straightforward to correct.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Ramping up too quickly</h3>
              <p>A new treadmill invites a jump from little exercise to daily sessions. Muscles adapt quickly, but tendons, cartilage and bone take longer. Increase duration, frequency or intensity gradually, and change one at a time.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Running the same session every day</h3>
              <p>Identical speed, incline and duration day after day means thousands of identical strides. Varying sessions across the week — easy walks, incline walks, easy runs at different paces — spreads the load across tissues.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Treating decline as a gentle option</h3>
              <p>Downhill walking and running make the thigh muscles brake while lengthening, which loads the front of the knee more and causes notable soreness. It has uses for trained runners preparing for hilly races, but beginners and anyone with knee discomfort can leave it alone.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Pushing through warning signs</h3>
              <p>Swelling, locking, the knee giving way, or pain that worsens as you exercise are not things to train through. Stop and get the knee assessed by a doctor or physical therapist, who can tell you what is going on and what is safe.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For healthy knees, treadmills are not the problem.</strong> Research on recreational runners is broadly reassuring, and a treadmill deck is a
            little kinder than pavement. Knee trouble usually comes from doing too much too soon,
            monotony, overstriding, decline settings, worn shoes and leaning on the rails.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already have knee pain or a joint condition, talk to a doctor or physical
            therapist first, and consider walking, incline walking or an elliptical. Buying with
            your knees in mind? See our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmills for bad knees</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

